  "text": "Her son died on her lap and she got on a donkey and went to argue with a prophet.\n\nSecond Kings four. This woman is described as great. Standing, money, influence. She's the one who notices Elisha passing through and builds him a room. Bed, table, stool, candlestick.\n\nHe wants to repay her. She says no. She's fine.\n\nHe gives her a son anyway.\n\nYears later the boy's out in the field, says \"My head, my head,\" and is carried to his mother. And the text says he sat on her knees till noon, and then died.\n\nNow watch her.\n\nShe carries him upstairs, lays him on the prophet's bed, shuts the door, and calls for a donkey. Her husband asks why she's going, it isn't new moon or sabbath. She says, \"It shall be well.\"\n\nShe does not tell him the child is dead. She's not asking permission, she's going.\n\nShe rides to Carmel. Elisha's servant tries to intercept her with polite questions. She blows straight past him.\n\nThen she catches Elisha by the feet. And Gehazi moves to shove her off, and Elisha says, \"Let her alone, for her soul is vexed within her.\"\n\nAnd she says, \"As the Lord liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not leave thee.\"\n\nHe got up and followed her. Faith can look like refusing to be managed.",
